---
/* WHO IS ASKING YOU TO TRUST THEM.
 *
 * A site that tells people their luggage will be refused is asking to be
 * believed, and "who made this and why" is a fair question to put to it. That
 * is what this page is for — not a mission statement.
 *
 * THE NUMBERS ARE DERIVED, NEVER TYPED. Every count comes from
 * coverageStats(), the same function /methodology uses. A hand-written "we
 * cover 75 airlines" is true on the day it is written and quietly false a
 * month later, and being quietly false about our own coverage is precisely the
 * failure this site exists to complain about in other people.
 *
 * STUB, and honestly labelled as one at the foot. Frank: "They can be
 * imperfect for the moment but please stub them out."
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { coverageStats } from '../lib/data.mjs';

const s = await coverageStats();
---
<Base
  title="About — Sizer"
  description="Who makes Sizer, what it measures, and the things it deliberately does not tell you."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-page px-6">

    <section class="pt-12 pb-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">About</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-lg text-ink-2">
        Sizer answers one question: will this bag get past that airline's gate,
        on the fare you actually booked?
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Why it exists</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Luggage is sold on the packing space inside it. Airlines measure the
        outside. Wheels, handles and feet live in the gap between those two
        numbers, and that gap is why a bag sold as "carry-on size" gets stopped
        at the gate.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Almost every source for this is trying to sell you something. We are
        trying to answer the question.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What we hold</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        {s.carriers_researched} airlines, each read from the airline's own
        baggage page, and {s.bags_total} bags, of which {s.bags_sized} carry a
        dimension we are willing to publish.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        Only {s.bags_promotable} of those may be put forward as a
        recommendation. The rest are shown but never suggested, because their
        figures come from a review or from a manufacturer who never said
        whether wheels were counted. That distinction is the whole of
        <a href="/methodology/" class="text-blue hover:text-blue-hover hover:underline underline-offset-2">how we work it out</a>,
        and every figure is traceable from
        <a href="/sources/" class="text-blue hover:text-blue-hover hover:underline underline-offset-2">sources</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        We have measured exactly {s.bags_measured} bags ourselves. We say so
        rather than implying otherwise.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">What we don't do</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        We don't review luggage. Whether a bag lasts five years, whether the
        wheels survive cobblestones, whether the warranty gets honoured — none
        of that shows up in a dimension, and we have nothing useful to say
        about any of it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        We also can't promise a gate agent will behave the way a published
        policy says. Airlines enforce unevenly and change limits without
        notice. Our verdicts describe the published rule and how strictly that
        airline is known to apply it. They are not a guarantee.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Money</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        There is currently no advertising, no sponsorship and no affiliate
        programme on this site, and nobody pays to appear in it. If that
        changes, a disclosure will appear in the footer of every page — and it
        will appear because a programme is live, not because someone remembered
        to write it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Corrections</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2">
        If a figure here is wrong, we would rather know. An airline changing
        its limit, a manufacturer publishing a different number, a bag we have
        filed under the wrong measurement — all of it is worth reporting.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
        A contact address is not published yet. It is the next thing to fix on
        this page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="max-w-3xl border-t border-rule py-8 text-sm text-muted">
      This page is an early draft and deliberately incomplete.
    </p>

  </div>
</Base>
